// TEST: a block lambda whose open parameter is given a tuple (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// R10 remainder (the numbers-only rule, formalism 2.4): a block lambda's
// parameter its body leaves open is BOUND by a direct call's argument -- a Bool,
// a String, a struct, a sum-type value, an index value -- as a numeric one always
// was. It used to stay open, fall to zonk's Float64 default, and the call
// converted the argument silently. A tuple argument is not
// bound (the body was typed as a single value); the call is refused at the
// argument instead of converting it (the base died in g++).
// ============================================================================
let r = {
    let idl = lambda(x) -> x
    idl((true, 1))
}
// ERROR: BL3001 @ 12:9
// ERROR-CONTAINS: Annotate the parameter
